  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Every noun is masculine or feminine",
      "body": "In Spanish, every noun has a gender — even objects. The ending is usually your best clue:\n\n- Nouns ending in *-o* are usually masculine: *el libro* (book), *el vaso* (glass).\n- Nouns ending in *-a* are usually feminine: *la mesa* (table), *la ventana* (window).\n- Nouns ending in *-ción*, *-sión*, *-dad* are feminine: *la canción* (song), *la ciudad* (city).\n- Other endings (*-e*, consonants) must be learned: *el coche* (car, in Spain — *el carro* in much of Latin America), *la calle* (street), *el papel* (paper), *la luz* (light).\n\nA few common exceptions: *el día* (day) and *el mapa* (map) are masculine; *la mano* (hand) is feminine. Always learn a noun together with its article."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Definite articles: the",
      "body": "\"The\" has four forms, matching the noun's gender and number.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "",
          "Singular",
          "Plural"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Masculine",
            "*el libro*",
            "*los libros*"
          ],
          [
            "Feminine",
            "*la mesa*",
            "*las mesas*"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Indefinite articles: a, an, some",
      "body": "\"A/an\" also agrees. In the plural, *unos / unas* means \"some\" or \"a few\".",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "",
          "Singular",
          "Plural"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Masculine",
            "*un libro* — a book",
            "*unos libros* — some books"
          ],
          [
            "Feminine",
            "*una mesa* — a table",
            "*unas mesas* — some tables"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Making nouns plural",
      "body": "- Ending in a vowel: add *-s* — *casa → casas*, *coche → coches*.\n- Ending in a consonant: add *-es* — *papel → papeles*, *ciudad → ciudades*.\n- Ending in *-z*: change it to *-ces* — *luz → luces*, *lápiz → lápices*.\n- A written accent on the last syllable disappears when *-es* is added: *canción → canciones*.\n\nPeople: many nouns for people change *-o* to *-a* (*el amigo → la amiga*), and nouns ending in *-e* or *-ista* often just change the article (*el estudiante → la estudiante*, *el turista → la turista*)."
    }
  ],
